# text = Uppercross was a moderate-sized village , which a few years back had been completely in the old English style , containing only two houses superior in appearance to those of the yeomen and labourers ; the mansion of the squire , with its high walls , great gates , and old trees , substantial and unmodernized , and the compact , tight parsonage , enclosed in its own neat garden , with a vine and a pear-tree trained round its casements ; but upon the marriage of the young ' squire , it had received the improvement of a farm-house elevated into a cottage , for his residence , and Uppercross Cottage , with its veranda , French windows , and other prettiness , was quite as likely to catch the traveller's eye as the more consistent and considerable aspect and premises of the Great House , about a quarter of a mile farther on .
